Z||000000||afRaceR||07-29-2001||11:19 PM||||Ok.........I'm tired of this hydraulic system. I gotta get some help. I've had a weak clutch in this thing pretty much since I have owned it. This last week I replaced the master and slave cylinders and got it bleed. I thought I had it fixed. <P>Until today. I was driving adn then all the sudden I lost clutch - air bubble right? well, I tried for 30 minutes today to get it bleed and its just not there. I have tried every way I know to bleed it. I even tried archies method.<P>I just can't get it bled. I need some input here please. I know the cylinders are good, its air somewhere. <BR>This is what I'm thinking. making a tool that keeps the slave cylinder completely retracted. Bleed it like this until there's no air in the system. Then let the slave cylinder engage and it will only suck fluid in. <P>Also is there any known problems with the MC cap. I had no pressure in the clutch, I unscrewed it, pumped it a couple of times and the clutch built up pressure. its a new cap and a new diaphragm. I can't figure this out.<BR>Sorry this is so long.......I gotta get this fixed by Tuesday, I'm running out of ideas.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||07-29-2001||11:31 PM||||sorry but it still sounds like you say there is air in the systemdid you bench bleed the M/C.?I dont think there is any easy solution to your problem.you definetly have air in your system and you need to keep bleeding till you get it all out .somebody here may have a different solution .goodluck||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||07-30-2001||06:02 AM||||ditto on the bench bleeding. The easiest method I found to bleed the system is to remove the slave from the bracket and point the now extended rod down. Bleed the system like you would brakes, after you close the bleeder screw for the last time the clutch pedal shouldn't move at all(hydraulic lock). Then just reinstall the slave.||||reg||1
Z||000003||afRaceR||07-30-2001||08:20 PM||||Well....I rebled the breaks today. Again, right after I did it, perfect........then shortly went to **** .<BR>My dad's got a pressure bleeder, so I hooked that up, felt great. Again went to **** .<BR>Everytime I bled it, there was so much air in the system.<BR>I saw a little wet spot on the fitting on the line. Tell me how this sounds? The connecter is cracked just enough to let air enter the system but not realy enough to let fluid leak out.<BR>I have JB weld on it right now, hopefully it will get me to colorado springs tomorrow.<BR>And if anyone has the part number for the clutch line and maybe a ballpark price that would be greatly appreciated. Its an 85 6cyl, 4spd.<BR>||||reg||1
